  "notes": "A Bad Boy Hitmen producer-drummer-singer from the Winans gospel family, 1995–2008; the band is \"60–100 straight; ballads 60–76 half-time\", and the ballad half is the base with the mode at 70. The identifying drum claim is biographical and the research states it plainly: \"**he is a drummer first**, which shows in played-feel programming — kick on 1 with a pushed pickup, clap/snare on beat 3 at the ballad tempos, live-feel tambourine and hand percussion over the grid\". So the kick's secondary anchor is `1a` (the pushed pickup), the snare sits 5–12 ms late with real ghost activity, `quantizeStrength` falls to 0.56 and `velocityVar` rises to 0.3 — the loosest grid of the Hitmen-lineage producers here — and the perc bed is a four-lane tambourine/conga/shaker/ride set at three to seven hits a bar. Harmony is the family lineage: \"gospel four-part backgrounds over 4-chord cycles\" with \"functional major/minor with passing chords and modulation\", marked `[LOW-CONF]`, so `maxChords` is 6 with a passing `vii°` family and `susOrDimProb` raised, and the counter is `octave_echo` at weight 4 — that is what a four-part background stack is. \"His signature productions pair a flipped or interpolated loop with a live gospel vocal arrangement\", which is the model in one sentence: a loose, played kit under written vocal harmony. The low end is split — \"808 sub with long decay on the 2004–2008 records, organ pedal and played bass on the gospel-adjacent material\" — two bass behaviours rather than a drum-kit 808, so no `drums.bass808` block and a real bass part with a 0.2 glide. ⚠ Confidence is **low**: the research rates it medium-low, with the drum and harmony detail \"partly inferred\". ⚠ Every velocity and density number is INFERRED. ⚠ `session.keys` is INFERRED: the entry's Keys line gives \"gospel harmonic language from the Winans lineage — functional major/minor with passing chords and modulation [LOW-CONF]\" and enumerates no tonic, so the values here and their weights are this author's reading of that line.",
